1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,100
Imagine that tomorrow at
exactly nine in the morning,

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,160
every human being on earth disappears.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:10,620
No explosion, no warning,

4
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:12,800
no bodies left behind,

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,760
just eight billion
people, gone in an instant.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,700
A coffee cup falls to the floor,

7
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:23,960
an empty bus rolls across an intersection.

8
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:25,960
On a motorway,

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,800
vehicles collide while
others slow to a halt.

10
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:31,740
High above the clouds,

11
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:35,800
passenger aircraft continue along
their last programmed routes,

12
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:37,260
at least for a while.

13
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,620
And in the middle of a city, the
traffic lights keep changing.

14
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:46,360
Red, green, amber, for drivers
who are no longer there.

15
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,500
For a moment, the machinery
of civilization carries on.

16
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,860
But every machine has limits.

17
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,580
Every system needs energy, repairs,

18
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:00,240
or someone to make a decision.

19
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:03,940
This is not a prediction,
it's a thought experiment.

20
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:07,750
What would happen to the world
we built if there were suddenly

21
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,800
nobody left to run it?

22
00:01:12,864 --> 00:01:15,124
The first danger is movement.

23
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:20,334
Cars, trucks, trains, ships,
and aircraft don't all stop

24
00:01:20,384 --> 00:01:22,304
safely when their operators vanish.

25
00:01:23,084 --> 00:01:24,904
Some automated systems intervene.

26
00:01:25,684 --> 00:01:28,004
Others don't. There would be crashes,

27
00:01:28,704 --> 00:01:32,454
fires, and collisions, but not
in every vehicle, and not all at

28
00:01:32,504 --> 00:01:36,644
once. Electricity is more
complicated than a single switch.

29
00:01:37,344 --> 00:01:39,934
Power grids constantly
balance supply against demand.

30
00:01:39,984 --> 00:01:43,894
Protective equipment can disconnect
generators or sections of the

31
00:01:43,944 --> 00:01:46,904
network when conditions
move outside safe limits.

32
00:01:47,924 --> 00:01:49,744
Some regions might go dark quickly.

33
00:01:50,264 --> 00:01:53,904
Others could keep operating
for hours, or perhaps longer,

34
00:01:54,144 --> 00:01:57,064
depending on their generation
mix and automated controls.

35
00:01:57,644 --> 00:02:00,854
There would be no repair crews,
no grid operators coordinating

36
00:02:00,904 --> 00:02:04,324
recovery, and no one to
refuel backup generators.

37
00:02:05,144 --> 00:02:08,434
The lights in a hospital might stay
on after the surrounding streets

38
00:02:08,484 --> 00:02:12,514
go dark. Emergency power could
keep critical equipment running

39
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:15,484
for a time. But fuel is finite.

40
00:02:16,144 --> 00:02:17,604
And equipment can fail.

41
00:02:18,184 --> 00:02:21,974
At airports, empty terminals remain
illuminated until their local

42
00:02:22,024 --> 00:02:23,584
systems lose power.

43
00:02:24,004 --> 00:02:27,694
On factory floors, robots might
complete a cycle before shutting

44
00:02:27,744 --> 00:02:30,064
down. In data centers,

45
00:02:30,744 --> 00:02:33,384
batteries and generators
briefly keep servers alive.

46
00:02:33,844 --> 00:02:35,244
Then one by one,

47
00:02:35,804 --> 00:02:38,914
connections disappear. The
internet wouldn't switch off

48
00:02:38,964 --> 00:02:40,484
everywhere at the same instant.

49
00:02:41,004 --> 00:02:42,784
It would fragment as electricity,

50
00:02:43,344 --> 00:02:46,424
cooling and network equipment
failed in different places.

51
00:02:49,704 --> 00:02:52,984
Now imagine the sound of
a city without people.

52
00:02:53,264 --> 00:02:56,794
No conversation, no construction,
no engines deliberately

53
00:02:56,844 --> 00:03:00,994
starting, just alarms, wind,
running water, and the occasional

54
00:03:01,044 --> 00:03:03,644
machine still operating on its own.

55
00:03:04,424 --> 00:03:05,604
In some neighborhoods,

56
00:03:06,244 --> 00:03:09,514
fires started by crashes or unattended
cooking spread until they run

57
00:03:09,564 --> 00:03:13,644
out of fuel, encounter a
barrier, or a stopped by weather.

58
00:03:14,464 --> 00:03:17,794
Without firefighters, some
fires could become devastating.

59
00:03:17,844 --> 00:03:20,264
Others would burn out quickly.

60
00:03:20,964 --> 00:03:23,724
Water systems begin to
reveal another dependency.

61
00:03:24,444 --> 00:03:27,614
Many communities rely on
electrically powered pumps, treatment

62
00:03:27,664 --> 00:03:30,064
plants, and active pressure management.

63
00:03:30,744 --> 00:03:34,604
Gravity fed reservoirs may continue
supplying water for a while.

64
00:03:35,204 --> 00:03:39,864
Elsewhere, pressure drops when
pumps stop or storage runs down.

65
00:03:40,344 --> 00:03:42,324
Waste water is just as vulnerable.

66
00:03:42,824 --> 00:03:47,254
When pumping stations lose
power, sewage can back up or

67
00:03:47,304 --> 00:03:51,894
overflow. Exactly when and
where depends on local geography

68
00:03:51,944 --> 00:03:53,684
and the design of the network.

69
00:03:54,564 --> 00:03:57,184
There is no single countdown
for the world's cities.

70
00:03:57,744 --> 00:04:01,654
New York, Sydney, Tokyo, and
a small mountain town would

71
00:04:01,704 --> 00:04:04,884
experience different
failures at different speeds.

72
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:12,590
For the animals that lived alongside
us, the disappearance is not

73
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,400
an instant return to nature.

74
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:17,840
Millions of pets would
be trapped inside homes.

75
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:21,420
Some could escape through
open doors or broken windows.

76
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,080
Others could not.

77
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:27,970
Animals dependent on regular
food, water, or temperature control

78
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:30,000
would be especially vulnerable.

79
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:32,890
Farm animals face different challenges.

80
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:37,720
A hardy grazing animal with
access to water might survive.

81
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:42,290
An animal confined in a barn or
dependent on automated feeding

82
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:45,140
could be in serious trouble within days.

83
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,210
In the wild, some species might
benefit from quieter roads and

84
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,340
reduced hunting or disturbance.

85
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,420
But ecosystems wouldn't simply reset.

86
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:00,780
Domestic animals, invasive species,

87
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:05,680
fences, dams, and the altered
landscape would still be there.

88
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:09,792
Nature would inherit a world
that humans had already changed.

89
00:05:12,792 --> 00:05:17,332
Without maintenance, small
problems become large ones.

90
00:05:18,112 --> 00:05:21,032
Rain enters a building
through a damaged roof.

91
00:05:21,632 --> 00:05:23,392
Water seeps into cracks.

92
00:05:24,172 --> 00:05:28,372
In cold climates, freezing
and thawing widen them.

93
00:05:28,852 --> 00:05:32,892
Metal Corodes. Timber decays
where moisture persists.

94
00:05:33,672 --> 00:05:37,652
Plants take root in gutters,
walls, and abandoned lots.

95
00:05:38,392 --> 00:05:42,492
But don't imagine every skyscraper
collapsing in 12 months.

96
00:05:43,332 --> 00:05:45,752
Most substantial structures
would still stand.

97
00:05:46,432 --> 00:05:48,652
Their deterioration would be uneven,

98
00:05:49,272 --> 00:05:53,422
governed by construction quality,
climate, exposure, and the

99
00:05:53,472 --> 00:05:55,472
failures that happened before.

100
00:05:56,432 --> 00:05:59,352
Underground spaces tell another story.

101
00:05:59,952 --> 00:06:03,582
Subway tunnels and basements that
require pumping can flood when

102
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:04,692
the pumps stop.

103
00:06:05,272 --> 00:06:08,332
Other tunnels may remain
dry for much longer.

104
00:06:09,412 --> 00:06:11,372
Roads begin to change too.

105
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:14,732
Without traffic, there's
less mechanical wear.

106
00:06:15,672 --> 00:06:20,102
Yet drainage systems clog, roots
expand, and seasonal heat

107
00:06:20,152 --> 00:06:22,172
and cold keep working on the pavement.

108
00:06:23,112 --> 00:06:27,032
From above, a city might still
look familiar after a year.

109
00:06:27,632 --> 00:06:30,832
At street level, it would
feel profoundly different.

110
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:37,990
Ten years without people is enough
for some abandoned places to

111
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:42,580
look dramatically overgrown,
especially in warm and wet climates.

112
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:44,800
Grasses become shrubs.

113
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:47,500
Shrubs become young trees.

114
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:51,640
Vines spread across facades
where conditions allow.

115
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:56,710
Elsewhere, in deserts or harsh
polar regions, vegetation may

116
00:06:56,760 --> 00:07:00,730
barely advance at all. The
shape of the world also changes

117
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:02,940
because our activity has stopped.

118
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:07,090
There are no new emissions from
operating human industries, no

119
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:10,860
commercial fishing fleets, and
no fresh roads being built.

120
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,630
But the climate doesn't instantly
return to a pre -industrial

121
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:19,150
state. Carbon dioxide already
in the atmosphere and heat

122
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,660
stored in the oceans, continue to matter.

123
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:26,070
Some human -made pollution would
persist for decades, centuries,

124
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:30,370
or longer. Stopping the source
isn't the same thing as erasing

125
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:32,280
what has already been released.

126
00:07:35,472 --> 00:07:39,522
A century passes, now the
differences between buildings become

127
00:07:39,572 --> 00:07:43,192
striking. Some poorly protected
structures have failed.

128
00:07:43,912 --> 00:07:45,572
Others are still recognizable.

129
00:07:46,132 --> 00:07:49,922
Stone monuments, massive concrete
works and sheltered structures

130
00:07:49,972 --> 00:07:53,192
may outlast nearby houses by generations.

131
00:07:53,832 --> 00:07:57,902
Bridges deteriorate as water,
corrosion and vegetation attack

132
00:07:57,952 --> 00:08:02,032
vulnerable components. Some
collapse, others endure.

133
00:08:02,572 --> 00:08:06,052
In coastal areas, salt
accelerates corrosion.

134
00:08:06,492 --> 00:08:09,762
In earthquake zones, the next
major tremor could destroy

135
00:08:09,812 --> 00:08:12,872
buildings that survived
decades of quiet neglect.

136
00:08:13,832 --> 00:08:17,122
Forests reclaim suitable land,
but not according to a neat

137
00:08:17,172 --> 00:08:21,602
timetable. Wildfire, drought,
floods and changing temperatures

138
00:08:21,652 --> 00:08:23,952
all influence what grows back.

139
00:08:24,172 --> 00:08:27,102
And some landscapes
remain unmistakably human.

140
00:08:27,152 --> 00:08:31,952
Minds scar the ground, reservoirs
reshape river valleys,

141
00:08:32,352 --> 00:08:36,452
roads and embankments leave long
straight lines through terrain.

142
00:08:37,012 --> 00:08:40,822
Even when a city disappears
beneath vegetation, its foundations

143
00:08:40,872 --> 00:08:43,032
and altered soils remain.

144
00:08:46,344 --> 00:08:50,434
A thousand years after the last
person vanished, the world would

145
00:08:50,484 --> 00:08:52,364
not be a pristine wilderness.

146
00:08:53,084 --> 00:08:56,754
Many familiar buildings might be
ruined, but some would survive

147
00:08:56,804 --> 00:08:59,284
as fragments or substantial structures.

148
00:08:59,984 --> 00:09:02,404
Their fate would depend
on local conditions.

149
00:09:03,224 --> 00:09:07,084
Dry deserts can preserve things
that humid forests destroy.

150
00:09:07,684 --> 00:09:10,564
Buried objects can outlast exposed ones.

151
00:09:10,944 --> 00:09:15,504
Human traces would survive in
sediments, plastics, ceramics,

152
00:09:15,624 --> 00:09:20,054
glass, alloys, landfills, and
the chemical signatures of

153
00:09:20,104 --> 00:09:24,234
industrial activity. The landscape
would preserve evidence of

154
00:09:24,284 --> 00:09:28,424
agriculture, excavation, and
enormous changes to ecosystems.

155
00:09:29,324 --> 00:09:32,914
Even if forests covered
former city streets, a future

156
00:09:32,964 --> 00:09:36,984
investigator could still discover
foundations beneath the soil.

157
00:09:37,524 --> 00:09:40,364
They might find tunnels, carved stone,

158
00:09:40,944 --> 00:09:44,504
artificial materials, and
strange concentrations of metals.

159
00:09:45,204 --> 00:09:48,894
The most enduring evidence of
humanity might not be our tallest

160
00:09:48,944 --> 00:09:53,376
towers. It might be the changes
we left in the ground itself.

161
00:09:56,376 --> 00:09:59,946
And that brings us to the
unsettling part of this thought

162
00:09:59,996 --> 00:10:04,106
experiment. We tend to
think of civilization as

163
00:10:04,156 --> 00:10:08,856
permanent, because we live inside it.

164
00:10:09,636 --> 00:10:10,876
Roads look solid.

165
00:10:12,596 --> 00:10:13,876
Cities look timeless.

166
00:10:15,076 --> 00:10:18,116
The systems around us feel automatic,

167
00:10:18,936 --> 00:10:23,286
but they are maintained every
day by millions of people, often

168
00:10:23,336 --> 00:10:26,396
doing work that most of us never see.

169
00:10:27,296 --> 00:10:30,896
Remove those people and
the world doesn't end.

170
00:10:31,476 --> 00:10:35,416
It changes. Some things fail in hours.

171
00:10:36,416 --> 00:10:38,496
Some endure for centuries.

172
00:10:39,636 --> 00:10:43,866
And some marks we leave may
last far beyond anything we

173
00:10:43,916 --> 00:10:45,356
recognize as a city.

174
00:10:46,096 --> 00:10:50,026
A thousand years from now, if
another intelligence walked through

175
00:10:50,076 --> 00:10:54,206
the ruins, it might ask a
question that sounds strangely

176
00:10:54,256 --> 00:10:57,756
familiar. Who built all this?

177
00:10:58,456 --> 00:10:59,736
And where did they go?

